      "writtenOn": "2021-12-06T23:10:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not clear whether cur_port always represents the port we want to be setting/getting the mux on.  It\u0027s definitely the port which is suspended and resumed, so it should be the active port for all the commands which run in the disabled state.  But the mux get can occur in any TC state.  Having said that, there\u0027s only ever one last_result to return, so perhaps for all things we can consider there only being one port active/current.\n\nAdding some folks from Intel to clarify the flow here",
      "parentUuid": "13758405_87931701",
      "revId": "037c770149da8dcf76682da8909197a5b5df08b0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "19a3b4ac_016371a7",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_retimer_fw_update.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-06T21:42:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This might be out of scope, but I don\u0027t really know. I don\u0027t see that access to last_result (via this function and usb_retimer_fw_update_get_result) is explicitly synchronized. Maybe it is effectively synchronized by thread priority.\n\nIf there is a race window between writing to last_result and reading it, it seems like this CL potentially makes the window longer. How does the reader of last_result know that the value applies to the most recently executed operation?\n\nIf this is a real issue, you might be able to avoid it by moving the wait loop into usb_retime_fw_update_get_result, which is called by a host command. It could block until the value of last_result is meaningful, assuming that wouldn\u0027t be too terribly long for the AP.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 111,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 111,
        "endChar": 12
      },
      "revId": "037c770149da8dcf76682da8909197a5b5df08b0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9e70b788_f0f7c4cc",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_retimer_fw_update.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-06T23:10:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I believe usb_retimer_fw_update_get_result() is run in the ACPI code, which is called in the interrupt context of our chip code.  So blocking there is likely not feasible.\n\nI think the current synchronization is that the ACPI code is running at interrupt priority, and during the interrupt the last_result is filled in to an invalid value of USB_RETIMER_FW_UDPATE_INVALID_MUX.  Then I\u0027d assume the AP is essentially polling for the last_result to be something not-invalid before sending the next command.\n\nIntel, definitely correct me if I\u0027m wrong here.",
